WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.780
In 1846, mothers in one Vienna clinic died about four times as often as in the

2
00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:13.520
clinic next door. What could the doctors have been carrying?

3
00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:19.580
In the deadly clinic, doctors and students often handled bodies in autopsies.

4
00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:22.100
Then they examined mothers.

5
00:00:23.183 --> 00:00:26.503
Ignaz Semmelweis suspected the examiners' hands.

6
00:00:27.763 --> 00:00:30.723
In 1847 he had them wash in chlorine.

7
00:00:32.083 --> 00:00:34.283
You will test his records yourself in the lesson.

8
00:00:35.463 --> 00:00:37.183
But notice what he did not know.

9
00:00:38.283 --> 00:00:40.323
He could not name what was on those hands.

10
00:00:41.417 --> 00:00:43.497
Germ theory later gave the answer.

11
00:00:44.877 --> 00:00:49.537
Many diseases are caused by microbes, living things too small to see without a

12
00:00:49.537 --> 00:00:55.137
microscope. A microbe or virus that causes disease is called a pathogen.

13
00:00:56.537 --> 00:00:59.657
Many pathogens multiply once they get into the body.

14
00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:04.490
One becomes two, two become four, and the doubling keeps going.

15
00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:07.530
Meanwhile the body builds a defense.

16
00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:12.810
Antibodies are proteins that stick to one small part of an antigen.

17
00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:16.050
That can block a microbe or its toxin.

18
00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:21.950
The first time the body meets a pathogen, building them often takes a week or more.

19
00:01:22.983 --> 00:01:25.143
So picture an infection as a race.

20
00:01:26.703 --> 00:01:29.123
Microbes multiply, and defenses catch up.

21
00:01:30.263 --> 00:01:33.883
In this simple model, the microbes fall once the gold bar arrives.

22
00:01:35.103 --> 00:01:36.943
But by then they have climbed very high.

23
00:01:38.223 --> 00:01:41.543
In many infections, more microbes means a sicker person.

24
00:01:42.633 --> 00:01:44.353
There are two ways to get ahead.

25
00:01:45.573 --> 00:01:48.293
The first is to start the race with fewer microbes.

26
00:01:49.593 --> 00:01:54.313
Hand washing, clean water and sterile tools keep microbes out or leave fewer.

27
00:01:55.653 --> 00:01:57.633
Run the race again with a smaller start.

28
00:01:58.853 --> 00:02:02.293
The defense still arrives on the same day, but the peak is lower.

29
00:02:03.417 --> 00:02:06.297
The second way is to send the defense in early.

30
00:02:07.637 --> 00:02:12.197
A vaccine shows the immune system an antigen, or instructions to make one.

31
00:02:13.357 --> 00:02:18.617
The immune system learns to recognize a pathogen or its toxin without the full

32
00:02:18.617 --> 00:02:24.277
disease. During that first meeting, some immune cells become memory cells.

33
00:02:25.097 --> 00:02:27.157
They stay in the body long after.

34
00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:31.760
Now the real pathogen arrives, carrying the same antigen.

35
00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:34.820
This is the body's second meeting.

36
00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:39.980
Will the gold defense arrive sooner or later than it did the first time?

37
00:02:46.467 --> 00:02:53.287
Sooner. Memory cells recognize the antigen quickly, and they lead to large amounts

38
00:02:53.287 --> 00:02:57.127
of antibody. The defense arrives days earlier.

39
00:02:58.407 --> 00:03:02.327
The microbes have had less time to double, so they never climb very high.

40
00:03:03.367 --> 00:03:04.947
Hygiene shrinks the start.

41
00:03:06.167 --> 00:03:08.247
A vaccine moves the defense earlier.

42
00:03:09.447 --> 00:03:11.347
They are two ways to win the same race.

43
00:03:12.587 --> 00:03:15.767
A smaller start or an earlier defense keeps the peak low.
